<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gallipoli</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gelibolu, Gallipoli peninsula, Thracian Chersonese</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gallipoli (Turkish Gelibolu) is a town and peninsula on the European shore of the Dardanelles in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, known in antiquity as the Thracian Chersonese. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> seized the town in 1354 after an earthquake damaged its walls, gaining their first lasting foothold in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. In 1915–1916 the peninsula was the scene of the Allied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli-campaign">Gallipoli campaign</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gallipoli.jpg" alt="Gallipoli" width="320" height="210" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gallipoli_peninsula_from_space.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Gallipoli Peninsula lies in the southern part of East Thrace, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean Sea</a> to the west and the Dardanelles to the east. Gallipoli is the Italian form of the Greek name Kallípolis, meaning beautiful city, the original name of the modern town of Gelibolu. In antiquity the peninsula was protected by the Long Wall across its narrowest part, an isthmus of about 6.5 km, and it was known for its wheat. It lay on the main route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and controlled the shipping route from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>. The city of Sestos was the main crossing point on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespont">Hellespont</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> the Thracian Dolonci held the peninsula before Greek settlers, mainly Ionian and Aeolian, founded about twelve cities there in the 7th century BCE. Miltiades the Elder founded a major Athenian colony around 560 BCE. Athens enrolled the peninsula in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delian-league">Delian League</a> in 478 BCE, and it was ceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a> in 338 BCE. Lysimachus later made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachia">Lysimachia</a> his capital there. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> took it and gave it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eumenes-ii">Eumenes II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a> in 188 BCE; from 129 BCE it belonged to the Roman province of Asia. It was part of the Eastern Roman Empire from 395, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila the Hun</a> captured Callipolis and Sestus in 443.</p><p>An earthquake destroyed the town and its walls on the night of 1 and 2 March 1354. Within a month the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> besieged and captured it, making it their first stronghold in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the staging area for expansion across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>. The Savoyard Crusade recaptured it for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> in 1366, but it was handed back in September 1376. British and French forces used it as an encampment during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a> in 1854. In 1915 the Ottoman Empire deported Greek residents from Gallipoli and the surrounding region before the Allied landings.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, French, British and other forces fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli-campaign">Gallipoli campaign</a> of 1915 and 1916 to secure a sea route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. The first Australian troops landed at ANZAC Cove on 25 April 1915, and the last Allied soldiers withdrew by 9 January 1916. Turks regard the campaign as a defining moment in their national history, and 25 April is ANZAC Day in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>. Greek troops occupied the town on 4 August 1920 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-turkish-war-1919-1922">Greco-Turkish War</a>, and it returned to Turkish rule on 26 November 1922. From November 1920 to May 1922 a camp near Gelibolu held White Russian exiles, 26,590 of them transferred from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Between 1923 and 1926 Gallipoli was the centre of Gelibolu Province. It later became a district centre in Çanakkale Province. The Gallipoli Peninsula Historical National Park preserves battlefields, cemeteries and memorials of the campaign.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gallipoli” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The final century and a half was one of steady Ottoman encirclement: the Ottomans established themselves in Europe at <b>Gallipoli</b> in 1354, moved their capital to Adrianople (Edirne), and reduced the empire to the city itself and scattered remnants in Greece.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Cotys I fought Athens for the <b>Thracian Chersonese</b> in the 360s BCE, but divided Thracian forces could not withstand the reformed army of Philip II of Macedon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/odrysian-kingdom">Odrysian Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Meanwhile the Ottomans, established at <b>Gallipoli</b> from 1354, advanced through Thrace.</blockquote><a href="/empire/second-bulgarian-empire">Second Bulgarian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>His defence of <b>Gallipoli</b> in World War I made his military career.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/kemalism">Kemalism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The earliest known recording is in The Landing of the Australian Troops in Egypt (c. 1916), a dramatised record of Australian troops on their way to <b>Gallipoli</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/advance-australia-fair">Advance Australia Fair</a></li><li><blockquote>What a Lovely War, <b>Gallipoli</b> (1981) and Das Boot (1981), and it forms part of the regimental march of Princess Patricia's Canadian Light Infantry.</blockquote><a href="/songs/its-a-long-way-to-tipperary">It's a Long Way to Tipperary</a></li></ul></section>
